WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.518
Unauthorized-access hunts examine whether people or systems reached OT assets outside approved identity, path, time, or privilege.

00:00:08.518 --> 00:00:17.957
Start with the authorized-access model: which vendors, employees, service accounts, jump hosts, VPNs, destinations, and maintenance windows are expected.

00:00:17.957 --> 00:00:25.554
Review remote-access and VPN sessions for unusual source systems, locations, login times, duration, and targets.

00:00:25.554 --> 00:00:32.230
Correlate jump-host activity with identity events, ticket approval, firewall connections, and destination logs.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:37.986
A successful session without a corresponding work record may require validation.

00:00:37.986 --> 00:00:40.518
Shared accounts complicate attribution.

00:00:40.518 --> 00:00:50.878
Hunters should identify where they remain, whether use can be tied to an individual session, and whether privilege exceeds the task.

00:00:50.878 --> 00:00:57.554
Service accounts deserve separate baselines because interactive or cross-zone use may be unexpected.

00:00:57.554 --> 00:01:08.835
Look for access that bypasses managed entry points, originates from an unapproved workstation, reaches an unusual asset, or continues beyond the authorized window.

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:17.813
Failed logons, privilege changes, and access after account expiration can add context, but none proves intent by itself.

00:01:17.813 --> 00:01:22.188
Coordinate findings with operations and the access owner.

00:01:22.188 --> 00:01:27.483
Emergency support, outages, or field work may explain unusual timing.

00:01:27.483 --> 00:01:36.001
Preserve session and approval evidence before it expires, and avoid terminating active access without understanding process consequence.

00:01:36.001 --> 00:01:43.137
The hunt should conclude with affected accounts and assets, evidence, confidence, and recommended follow-up.

00:01:43.137 --> 00:01:51.195
Findings may improve access policy, account ownership, monitoring, vendor procedures, and detections for future unauthorized pathways.
